8. What is the useContext hook and when should you use it?

useContext is a Hook that lets you access data from a React Context directly in a component — without having to pass props manually at every level.

It helps you share data easily between components without "prop drilling".

🧩 What is Prop Drilling?

Prop drilling means passing data from a parent → to child → to grandchild, even when only the deepest child needs it.

Example (without context):

function App() {
const username = "Prem";
return <Parent username={username} />;
}

function Parent({ username }) {
return <Child username={username} />;
}

function Child({ username }) {
return <h1>Hello, {username}</h1>;
}

Here, the username prop has to be passed through every layer, even if only Child needs it — this becomes messy in large apps.

⚙️ React Context + useContext = Easy Sharing

With Context, you can store shared data in one place,
and with useContext, you can easily access it from any component — no prop drilling needed.

🧠 Example: Using useContext
import React, { createContext, useContext } from "react";

// 1️⃣ Create the context
const UserContext = createContext();

// 2️⃣ Provide the context value
function App() {
return (
<UserContext.Provider value="Prem">
<Child />
</UserContext.Provider>
);
}

// 3️⃣ Consume the context value
function Child() {
const username = useContext(UserContext); // access context directly
return <h1>Hello, {username}!</h1>;
}

✅ Output:
Hello, Prem!

🧩 When to use useContext
Use it when:
You need to share data across multiple components, especially at different levels of the component tree.

Example use cases:
Current user info (login data)
Theme (light/dark mode)
Language or localization settings
Global app settings or preferences

⚠️ When not to use it

Avoid overusing context for data that changes very frequently (like input values or counters),
because it can cause unnecessary re-renders in all components using that context.

✅ In short:
Concept Description
useContext React Hook that lets you read data from a context directly.
Purpose Avoids prop drilling — passes data without going through every component.
Used with createContext() and <Context.Provider>
When to use For global data like user, theme, or language.

👉 Simple summary:
useContext = “A shortcut to access shared data (context) anywhere in your component tree — without passing props manually.”

