11. 🧩 What is useMemo in React?

useMemo is a React Hook that helps you optimize performance by remembering (caching) the result of an expensive calculation — so React doesn’t have to redo it every time the component re-renders.

👉 In simple terms:

useMemo “remembers” the output of a function until its dependencies change.

🧠 Syntax
const memoizedValue = useMemo(() => {
// some expensive calculation
return result;
}, [dependencies]);

The function inside useMemo runs only when something in the dependency array changes.

Otherwise, React uses the cached value from the previous render.

import React, { useState, useMemo } from "react";

function WithUseMemo() {
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
const [text, setText] = useState("");

const expensiveCalculation = (num) => {
console.log("Calculating...");
let total = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < 1000000000; i++) {
total += i;
}
return total + num;
};

// ✅ useMemo caches the result until `count` changes
const total = useMemo(() => expensiveCalculation(count), [count]);

return (

<div>
<p>Expensive Result: {total}</p>
<button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment ({count})</button>
<input value={text} onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)} />
</div>
);
}

export default WithUseMemo;
🧠 Now:

The expensive function runs only when count changes.
Typing in the input (text) won’t trigger it again.

✅ Result: Better performance 🚀

🔍 Let’s break it step by step
🟢 Step 1: Initial render
useMemo runs
"Calculating..." logs
👉 Total runs = 1

🟢 Step 2: Click Increment (count changes)
Dependency [count] changed
useMemo runs again
👉 Total runs = 2

🔴 Step 3: Type in input (text changes)
Now:
setText(e.target.value)
Component re-renders ✅
But count is still same ❗
Dependency [count] did NOT change
👉 So React says:
“I already calculated this before… I’ll reuse it.”
👉 No new execution happens
So:
Previous total is reused (cached)
"Calculating..." is NOT printed

👉 New runs added = 0

