21. What is the difference between derived state and computed state in React?

🧠 1. Derived State (from Props or Other State)

➡️ Derived state means a piece of state that is based on (or copied from) another state or prop.
You’re storing it separately in the component — even though you could calculate it from existing data.

🧩 Example of Derived State
function Cart({ items }) {
// ❌ Derived state — total can be calculated from items
const [total, setTotal] = useState(0);

useEffect(() => {
const newTotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);
setTotal(newTotal);
}, [items]);

return <p>Total: ₹{total}</p>;
}

Here, total is derived from items.
We’re storing it in state unnecessarily — which can cause bugs or out-of-sync issues if we forget to update it.

✅ Better way: Compute it directly instead of storing it:

const total = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);

⚙️ 2. Computed State

➡️ Computed state is not stored in useState.
Instead, it’s calculated on the fly (derived at render time) based on other state or props.

🧩 Example of Computed State
function Cart({ items }) {
// 🧠 items is the only real state/prop
const total = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0); // computed

return <p>Total: ₹{total}</p>;
}

Here:
total is computed, not stored.
It always stays in sync with items.
No extra state or side effect is needed.

