27. 🧩 What is Code Splitting?

Normally, when you build a React app, all your code is bundled together into one big JavaScript file (bundle.js).
✅ That means:
When a user opens your app, the browser downloads the entire bundle — even the code for pages or features they might never visit.
❌ This makes your app slow to load initially.
⚙️ Code Splitting fixes this.
It means splitting your code into smaller chunks (files) that load only when needed.
So instead of one big bundle, you get:

main.js (for homepage)
about.js (for About page)
profile.js (for Profile page)

When the user goes to /profile, React loads only profile.js — on demand.
This reduces the initial loading time and improves performance. 🚀

