28. 🧠 What is Lazy Loading?

Lazy loading is how React implements code splitting.
It means React loads a component only when it’s actually needed, instead of at the beginning.

🪄 Example (Without Lazy Loading)
import Profile from './Profile'; // always loaded

function App() {
return <Profile />;
}

Here, the Profile component loads immediately, even if the user never visits it. ❌

✅ Example (With Lazy Loading)
import React, { Suspense, lazy } from 'react';

const Profile = lazy(() => import('./Profile'));

function App() {
return (
<Suspense fallback={<p>Loading...</p>}>
<Profile />
</Suspense>
);
}

🧠 What happens:
React doesn’t load Profile until it’s actually rendered.
While loading, React shows the fallback (Loading...).
Once it’s downloaded, React displays the component.

⚡ Why It’s Useful
✅ Benefits of Code Splitting + Lazy Loading:

Faster initial page load 🚀
Less data downloaded upfront
Improved user experience
Scales better for large apps

🧾 In short (Interview Answer):
Code splitting means breaking your app’s code into smaller bundles that load only when needed.
Lazy loading is the technique React uses to load components on demand — improving performance and reducing initial load time.

